      "slurm": "#!/bin/sh\n#PBS -q i1accs\n#PBS -l select=1:ncpus=64:mpiprocs=1:ompthreads=1\n#PBS -l walltime=00:15:00\n#PBS -N GCNLMP\n##PBS -I\n\nmodule purge\nmodule load openmpi_nvhpc/4.1.2\nmodule load nvhpc-nompi/22.2_cuda11.6\n\nsource ~/miniconda3/etc/profile.d/conda.sh\nconda activate nequip_GPU2\n\nexport CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"0\"\n\n./lmp -in input.data\n",
      "flux": "#!/bin/sh\n#FLUX: --queue=i1accs\n\n# The PBS select statement requests 64 CPUs for a single process. This is translated to a request for an exclusive node in Flux,\n# which will provide all available cores (at least 64) for the job. This approach is chosen based on frequent validation failures with specific task/cpu directives.\n#FLUX: --nodes=1\n#FLUX: --exclusive\n\n#FLUX: --time=00:15:00\n#FLUX: --job-name=GCNLMP\n\n# The PBS directive for an interactive job (-I) is not applicable to a batch script and has been omitted.\n\nmodule purge\nmodule load openmpi_nvhpc/4.1.2\nmodule load nvhpc-nompi/22.2_cuda11.6\n\nsource ~/miniconda3/etc/profile.d/conda.sh\nconda activate nequip_GPU2\n\n# The CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES setting is preserved, but in a Flux job with a GPU request (not present here), Flux typically manages this automatically.\nexport CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"0\"\n\n./lmp -in input.data\n",
